## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical, beginner-friendly construction of a data pipeline with common tasks: deduplication, validation, timestamp handling, and partitioned Parquet, plus testing and monitoring.

## Key Concepts
- JSON Lines parsing
- Idempotent deduplication
- Data quality checks (required fields, types)
- Timestamp normalization to UTC
- Parquet writing and date partitioning
- Basic testing and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const readline = require('readline');
(async () => {
  const rl = readline.createInterface({ input: fs.createReadStream('logs.jsonl'), crlfDelay: Infinity });
  const seen = new Set();
  for await (const line of rl) {
    const obj = JSON.parse(line);
    if (!obj.user_id || !obj.timestamp || !obj.path || !obj.status) continue;
    const key = `${obj.timestamp}|${obj.user_id}|${obj.path}`;
    if (seen.has(key)) continue;
    seen.add(key);
    // normalization and persistence would occur here
  }
})();
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this for multi-region logs?
- What metrics and alerts would you add for production reliability?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend streaming and batch processing with a practical ML feature store, including contracts, versioning, lineage, and schema evolution—common in large tech orgs.

## Key Concepts
- Lakehouse (Iceberg) with feature tables
- Versioned feature registry and per-record data contracts
- Real-time (Flink) vs batch (Spark) feature computation
- Idempotent upserts and late-data handling
- End-to-end data lineage and monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
function validateRecord(record, schema) {
  // naive contract: required keys and types
  for (const [k, t] of Object.entries(schema.fields)) {
    if (!(k in record)) return { ok: false, error: `missing ${k}` };
    if (typeof record[k] !== t) return { ok: false, error: `${k} type` };
  }
  return { ok: true };
}
```

